<!--

Do not change instances of "Washington, D.C." to "District of Columbia" without discussing this first. District of Columbia may be the formal name of the district, but "Washington, D.C." has been used on the district's standard passenger plates since 1965 (except for 2013–17).

-->
The U.S. [[federal district]] of [[Washington, D.C.]], first required its residents to register their [[motor vehicle]]s in 1903. Registrants provided their own [[Vehicle registration plate|license plate]]s for display until 1907, when the district began to issue plates.<ref>{{cite web |title=Old District of Columbia License Plates |url=http://leatherlicenseplates.com/old-district-of-columbia-license-plates-vintage-district-of-columbia-license-plates/ |publisher=LeatherLicensePlates.com |date=<!-- Deny Citation Bot--> |access-date=September 27, 2017}}</ref> Plates are issued by the [[District of Columbia Department of Motor Vehicles]] (DC DMV). Front and rear plates are required for most classes of vehicles, while only rear plates are required for motorcycles and trailers.

==Passenger baseplates==
===1907 to 1966===
In 1956, the United States, Canada, and Mexico came to an agreement with the [[American Association of Motor Vehicle Administrators]], the [[Automobile Manufacturers Association]] and the [[National Safety Council]] that standardized the size for license plates for vehicles (except those for [[motorcycle]]s) at {{convert|6|in|cm}} in height by {{convert|12|in|cm}} in width, with standardized mounting holes.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Garrish |first=Christopher |date=October 2016 |title=Reconsidering the Standard Plate Size |magazine=Plates |publisher=[[Automobile License Plate Collectors Association]] |volume=62 |number=5}}</ref> The first Washington, D.C. license plate that complied with these standards was issued three years beforehand, in 1953 (dated March 31, 1954).

